   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the banking law, in relation to allowing electronic delivery of notices by premium finance agencies with insured consent",
    "basePrintNo": "S5061",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5061-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §554, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Banking Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5061\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the banking law, in relation to\nallowing electronic delivery of notices by premium finance agencies\nwith insured consent\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo permit premium finance agency notices to be delivered\nelectronically with insured consent.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 adds new subdivision ten to Section 554 of the Banking Law\nadding a definition of \"mail\" to permit delivery of notices by premium\nfinance agencies by electronic means (email and fax) to insureds with\ntheir consent and conspicuous subject matter in addition to more\ntraditional forms of delivery such as U.S.P.S. 1st class mail,\npersonal delivery and overnight delivery service.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThere has been an increasing trend in today's technologically advanced\nsociety toward communicating electronically.  The Legislature has\nadvanced bills promoting this trend (see S.5249B/A8076B and S2367B of\n2015/16). Premium Finance Agencies would like to provide the option to\ntheir customers of receiving notices and documents electronically.\n\nCurrently, the Department of Financial Services has advised that the\nuse of electronic communications in insurance related transaction is\ngenerally permitted, as long consumers have consented to doing\nbusiness electronically. This bill would codify this existing practice\nand incorporate long standing federal consumer protections for\ninsureds contained in 15 U.S. Code § 7001(c) including, but not\nlimited to:\n\n*affirmative consumer/insured consent that demonstrates the\nconsumer/insured can access the electronic information in the form\nthat will be used for delivery.\n\n*prior to consent, the consumer/insured is provided with clear\ninformation regarding (I) their right to have notices sent in\nnon-electronic form; (2) their right to withdraw such consent at any\ntime and directions on how to withdraw consent; (3) their right to\nlimit such consent to particular notices if so desired; and (4) the\nhardware and software requirements necessary to receive documents\nelectronically.\n\n*Hardware and software changes made after consent is received must be\nreported to the consumer/insured at which time consent can be revoked.\n\nIn order to provide final further protection, all \"mail\" sent to the\nconsumer/insured in the form of email or fax must conspicuously and\nconcisely notice the subject matter of the email or fax.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nSimilar to S.6771 of 2016.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
