    "lawCode": "Amd §554, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Banking Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5061A\n\nSPONSOR: SEWARD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the banking law, in relation to allow-\ning electronic delivery of notices by premium finance agencies with\ninsured consent\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo permit premium finance agency notices to be delivered electronically\nwith insured consent.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds new subdivision ten to Section 554 of the Banking Law\nadding a definition of \"mail\" to permit delivery of notices by premium\nfinance agencies by electronic means (email and fax) to insureds with\ntheir consent and conspicuous subject matter in addition to more tradi-\ntional forms of delivery such as U.S.P.S. 1st class mail, personal\ndelivery and overnight delivery service.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThere has been an increasing trend in today's technologically advanced\nsociety toward communicating electronically.  The Legislature has\nadvanced bills promoting this trend (see S.5249B/A8076B and S2367B of\n2015/16). Premium Finance Agencies would like to provide the option to\ntheir customers of receiving notices and documents electronically.\n\nCurrently, the Department of Financial Services has advised that the use\nof electronic communications in insurance related transaction is gener-\nally permitted, as long consumers have consented to doing business elec-\ntronically. This bill would codify this existing practice and incorpo-\nrate long standing federal consumer protections for insureds contained\nin 15 U.S. Code § 7001(c) including, but not limited to:\n\n*affirmative consumer/insured consent that demonstrates the\nconsumer/insured can access the electronic information in the form that\nwill be used for delivery.\n\n*prior to consent, the consumer/insured is provided with clear informa-\ntion regarding (1) their right to have notices sent in non-electronic\nform; (2) their right to withdraw such consent at any time and\ndirections on how to withdraw consent; (3) their right to limit such\nconsent to particular notices if so desired; and (4) the hardware and\nsoftware requirements necessary to receive documents electronically.\n\n*Hardware and software changes made after consent is received must be\nreported to the consumer/insured at which time consent can be revoked.\n\nIn order to provide final further protection, all \"mail\" sent to the\nconsumer/insured in the form of email or fax must conspicuously and\nconcisely notice the subject matter of the email or fax.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nSimilar to 5.6771 of 2016.\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
